<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad II of Granada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1235">1235</a>–<a href="/years/1302">1302</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad II al-Faqih · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad II was the second Nasrid sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, reigning from 1273 to 1302. He organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s administration and defended it by playing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Aragon and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> against one another. He welcomed North African volunteer troops into Granada's service and secured the kingdom against Castilian pressure.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad was born in 633 AH, 1235 or 1236 CE, into the Nasrid clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjona">Arjona</a>, which according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib">Ibn al-Khatib</a> descended from Sa'd ibn Ubadah, a companion of the Islamic prophet Muhammad. His father, Muhammad I, made Arjona independent in 1232 and built a state centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. After the death of his elder brother Faraj in 1257, Muhammad and his brother Yusuf were named heirs, and with Yusuf's death he became sole heir. He served for a time as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, and when his father died after a fall from his horse on 22 January 1273 he was 38 and experienced in war and diplomacy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> lay between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, which demanded a tribute of 300,000 maravedís, about half its revenue, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, while the Banu Ashqilula, former allies of the Nasrids, held Málaga in rebellion. In late 1273 Muhammad promised Alfonso X vassalage and tribute in return for an end to Castilian support for the rebels, but Alfonso took the money and kept backing them. Muhammad turned to the Marinid sultan Abu Yusuf Yaqub, whose army won at Ecija in September 1275, though he was offended to be treated as the equal of the Banu Ashqilula.</p><p>When the Banu Ashqilula handed Málaga to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a>, Muhammad encouraged Yaghmurasen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> to attack them, and regained the city on 31 January 1279. In 1280 he faced Castile, the Marinids and the Banu Ashqilula at once, but held out with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteers-of-the-faith">Volunteers of the Faith</a> and the rift between Alfonso and his son Sancho. In 1288 the Banu Ashqilula left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Castile took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a> in 1292 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>'s help but kept it, and a joint siege with the Marinids in 1294 failed. After Sancho's death in 1295 Muhammad took Quesada and, in 1299, Alcaudete.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 8 April 1302, before a planned offensive with Aragon, and was succeeded by his son Muhammad III; allegations that his son had poisoned him were never confirmed. He instituted the Nasrid royal protocol and the court chancery, built frontier strongholds held by governors whom the court appointed, and enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>, where he built the Tower of the Ladies and the Tower of the Points. His epithet al-Faqih reflects his learning: he wrote poetry and drew scholars such as Muhammad al-Riquti and Muhammad ibn al-Raqqam to his court.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad II of Granada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Muhammad II</b> and his successors played Castile, Aragon and the Marinids against one another, at times losing or recovering ports such as Tarifa, Algeciras and Gibraltar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Arjona, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.94,-4.06&amp;year=1278" aria-label="Arjona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4449 800 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Muhammad II of Granada"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4899 1070h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4899 1070h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.94,-4.06&amp;year=1278">What was here in 1278? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
